  "slug": "maternal-remodeling-efae",
  "summary": "Recent preprints connect pregnancy and lactation to maternal tissue remodeling and to maternal immune cell transfer to neonates.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that reproductive physiology reshapes maternal tissues and their immune compartments. In a mouse preprint, pregnancy-driven estradiol signaling expands intestinal regulatory T cells that secrete IL-10 and promote stem cell proliferation, remodeling the small intestine. A separate foster-nursing mouse study reports that maternal immune cells survive neonatal gut transit, enter circulation, and reach organs and injury sites in offspring, suggesting a route of systemic transfer during breastfeeding. A single-cell and spatial transcriptomic study of dairy goat mammary tissue across dry and peak lactation describes period-specific changes in epithelial, stromal and immune populations. The items differ in species, tissue and question; the goat study is descriptive profiling, and the transfer study concerns offspring rather than maternal remodeling. What is new is the joint attention to hormonal control of intestinal immune-epithelial remodeling, maternal-to-neonatal cell transfer, and lactation-stage mammary profiling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
